SmartCare v0.2 – Requirements Specification
1. Problem and scope
SmartCare Community Clinic currently uses spreadsheets, paper records, and manual processes to manage patients, practitioners, and appointments. This leads to duplicate bookings, difficulty locating patient records, inconsistent appointment status information, limited visibility of practitioner availability, manual cancellation processes, unreliable appointment history, and difficulty producing basic operational reports.

The scope of SmartCare v0.2 is to deliver a simple, centralised appointment booking system that supports patient, practitioner, and appointment management for a small clinic. It will replace fragmented manual processes, but will not attempt to be a full hospital information system.

2. Stakeholders
Stakeholder	Need	Evidence
Clinic management	Reduced errors, reliable reports, and a manageable system suitable for a small clinic.	Case study mentions operational problems and difficulty producing basic operational reports.
Reception staff	Easy booking, cancellation, and clear access to patient and appointment information.	Case study mentions duplicate bookings, manual cancellation, difficulty locating patient records.
Practitioners (GPs)	Clear visibility of their schedule and accurate appointment status.	Case study mentions limited visibility of practitioner availability and inconsistent appointment info.
Patients	Reliable appointments, fewer administrative mistakes, and clear communication.	Case study implies impact on patients through duplicate bookings and poor record management.
IT/system maintainer	A simple, maintainable, secure system that can be enhanced in stages.	Case study states iterative development and desire for a manageable application.


3. Functional requirements
FR-01: The system shall allow reception staff to create new patient records with basic demographic and contact information.
FR-02: The system shall allow reception staff to search and retrieve existing patient records by name, ID, or contact details.
FR-03: The system shall allow creation, update, and removal of practitioner (GP) profiles, including availability (days and times).
FR-04: The system shall allow reception staff to create new appointments by selecting a patient, practitioner, date, and time.
FR-05: The system shall prevent duplicate appointment bookings for the same practitioner at the same date and time.
FR-06: The system shall display practitioner availability and existing bookings in a calendar or schedule view.
FR-07: The system shall allow reception staff to update appointment status (e.g., booked, cancelled, completed, no-show).
FR-08: The system shall allow reception staff to cancel appointments and record the reason for cancellation.
FR-09: The system shall maintain an appointment history for each patient and practitioner.
FR-10: The system shall generate basic operational reports (e.g., number of appointments per day, cancellations, practitioner workload).
FR-11: The system shall support different user roles (e.g., receptionist, practitioner, admin) with appropriate access rights.
FR-12: The system shall allow practitioners to view their own schedule and associated patient appointment details.

4. Non-functional requirements
NFR-01: The system shall be easy to use for non-technical staff, with a simple and intuitive user interface.
NFR-02: The system shall respond to common operations (e.g., search, booking) within 2 seconds under normal load.
NFR-03: The system shall ensure patient and practitioner data is stored securely and protected from unauthorised access.
NFR-04: The system shall be reliable, with no data loss for confirmed appointments and records during normal operation.
NFR-05: The system shall be designed to allow incremental enhancements in future versions without major rework.
NFR-06: The system shall support backup and recovery procedures to restore data in case of system failure.

5. User stories
US-01: As a receptionist, I want to quickly create and update patient records, so that I can manage patient information accurately.

US-02: As a receptionist, I want to book appointments by selecting a patient and practitioner, so that I can schedule consultations efficiently.

US-03: As a receptionist, I want the system to prevent double-booking a practitioner, so that I avoid appointment conflicts.

US-04: As a practitioner, I want to view my daily and weekly schedule, so that I can plan my consultations.

US-05: As a clinic manager, I want to generate basic reports on appointments and cancellations, so that I can monitor clinic operations.

US-06: As a clinic manager, I want user roles and access control, so that sensitive patient data is only visible to authorised staff.

6. Acceptance criteria
Scenario 1 – Prevent duplicate bookings  
GIVEN a practitioner already has an appointment booked at a specific date and time
WHEN the receptionist attempts to create another appointment for the same practitioner at that date and time
THEN the system shall display an error message and prevent the duplicate booking.

Scenario 2 – View practitioner schedule  
GIVEN a practitioner is logged into the system
WHEN they open the schedule view for a selected day or week
THEN the system shall display all their appointments with patient names, times, and statuses.

Scenario 3 – Generate basic operational report  
GIVEN the clinic manager selects a date range and report type (e.g., appointments per day)
WHEN they request the report
THEN the system shall generate and display a summary of appointments, including counts and relevant statistics.

7. Assumptions and open questions
Assumptions:

A-01: The clinic operates with a small number of practitioners and patients, so performance requirements are modest.

A-02: The system will be used primarily by reception staff and practitioners within the clinic premises.

A-03: Integration with external systems (e.g., SMS/email reminders, payment systems) is out of scope for v0.2.

Open questions:

Q-01: What exact data fields are required for patient records (e.g., Medicare number, emergency contact)?

Q-02: What appointment status categories does the clinic want to standardise (e.g., rescheduled, in-progress)?

Q-03: Are there any regulatory or compliance requirements (e.g., specific privacy standards) that must be met?

Q-04: Should practitioners be able to modify their own availability, or is this restricted to admin/reception?

8. AI requirements review record
AI suggestion	Evidence?	Decision	Reason	Verification
Include user roles and access control as a functional requirement (FR-11).	Common practice for systems with sensitive data.	Accepted	Protects patient privacy and supports different stakeholder needs.	Confirm with client that roles and permissions are needed.
Add non-functional requirement for performance (NFR-02: 2-second response time).	General usability expectation for small systems.	Accepted (provisional)	Ensures system is responsive for reception workflows.	Measure response times during testing.
Exclude online payments and advanced features from v0.2 scope.	Not mentioned in case study; small clinic context.	Accepted	Keeps system simple and aligned with stated scope.	Validate with client that payments are out of scope.
Add reporting features as a core functional requirement (FR-10).	Case study explicitly mentions difficulty reporting.	Accepted	Directly addresses a stated operational problem.	Verify report types with clinic management.
Consider integration with SMS/email reminders in future versions only.	Not in case study; potential future enhancement.	Deferred	Could be useful but not essential for initial version.	Revisit in later stages after client consultation.


